5
00:00:17,647 --> 00:00:23,867
Imagine you are standing on a complete flat map, there are roads, coordinates and a tiny

6
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:29,707
dot marking your position but there is one thing the map doesn't tell you, how high

7
00:00:29,707 --> 00:00:30,747
the ground is.

8
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:36,787
Now suppose I give you a challenge, somewhere on this map is the lowest possible point,

9
00:00:37,367 --> 00:00:44,607
you can move left, right, forward or backward but without seeing height, how would you

10
00:00:44,607 --> 00:00:52,107
know which direction leads downhill. This is where contour plots became powerful. Start

11
00:00:52,107 --> 00:00:57,927
with a function of two variables. Instead of giving the function one number, we give

12
00:00:57,927 --> 00:01:06,587
it two, x and y. It returns a number we call z. Representing height, so every position

13
00:01:06,587 --> 00:01:13,887
on the map now has an elevation. Imagine lifting the map into three directions [dimensions]. Some

14
00:01:13,887 --> 00:01:21,147
places rise into hills, others sink into valleys. From the side, this landscape makes sense.

15
00:01:21,927 --> 00:01:29,227
But what if we need to show all that information on a flat sheet of paper? We slice the landscape

16
00:01:29,227 --> 00:01:35,547
with a horizontal plane. Think of slowly lowering a perfectly level sheet of glass through

17
00:01:35,547 --> 00:01:41,067
the surface. Wherever the sheet touches the landscape, those points have exactly the

18
00:01:41,067 --> 00:01:48,347
same height. Look straight down at the intersection. What you see is a contour line. A line joining

19
00:01:48,347 --> 00:01:54,967
locations where the function has one constant value. Lower the sheet again and you can get

20
00:01:54,967 --> 00:02:01,627
another contour. Each line tells you a different height. That is how a two dimensional map

21
00:02:02,167 --> 00:02:09,787
can describe a three dimensional world. Let's build with an actual equation. Our function

22
00:02:09,787 --> 00:02:18,007
is f of x and y equals the quantity x minus 1 squared plus the quantity y plus 2 squared.

23
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:25,907
It might look intimidating but notice something familiar. It's built from two square distances.

24
00:02:26,747 --> 00:02:32,347
The first measures how far x is from 1. The second measures how far y is from negative

25
00:02:32,347 --> 00:02:40,627
That tells us the center of this landscape is at the point 1, negative 2, not at 0, 0.

26
00:02:41,307 --> 00:02:49,287
So now you choose a fixed height which we will call C. To draw its contour, we set the

27
00:02:49,287 --> 00:02:57,307
equation equal to C. Our equation becomes the quantity x minus 1 squared plus the quantity

28
00:02:57,307 --> 00:03:05,227
y plus 2 squared equals c. This is the equation of the circle, centered at 1, negative 2.

29
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:13,907
The radius is the square root of c. So let's test it. When c is 1, the radius is 1. When

30
00:03:13,907 --> 00:03:21,747
c is 4, the radius is 2. And when c is 9, the radius is 3. 3 different heights, 3 different

31
00:03:21,747 --> 00:03:28,767
circles one common center. Notice that the radius doesn't glow [grow] by the same amount as

32
00:03:28,767 --> 00:03:34,647
the height. That's why we calculate the square root rather than simply asking [using] c as the radius.

33
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:40,927
There is another interesting detail. If c = 0, the circle shrinks to a single point

34
00:03:40,927 --> 00:03:48,507
1, negative 2. And if c < 0, there is no contour at all. Two square numbers can

35
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:54,067
never add up to something negative. So looking at the rings, we might guess that the center

36
00:03:54,067 --> 00:03:58,707
is the lowest point. But in mathematics, a picture is evidence not proof.

37
00:03:59,407 --> 00:04:04,847
Let's check using partial derivatives. The partial derivative with respect to x is 2 times

38
00:04:04,847 --> 00:04:11,327
the quantity x minus 1. The partial derivative with respect to y is 2 times the quantity y plus

39
00:04:11,327 --> 00:04:19,047
2. Set both equal to 0. The first tells x equals 1. The second tells us y equals negative

40
00:04:19,047 --> 00:04:25,627
2. So the gradient becomes 0 at 1, negative 2. Does that automatically make it a minimum?

41
00:04:26,507 --> 00:04:32,407
Not always. A saddle shaped surface can have a zero gradient at its center while rising

42
00:04:32,407 --> 00:04:38,027
in one direction and falling in another. We need one more argument. Look at the original

43
00:04:38,027 --> 00:04:43,807
function. It's the sum of two squares and the square of any real number is never negative.

44
00:04:44,527 --> 00:04:52,167
Therefore, our function can never go below zero at one negative two, both squares are exactly zero.

45
00:04:52,627 --> 00:04:58,007
So the function actually reaches that lowest possible value That proves something stronger than

46
00:04:58,007 --> 00:05:04,467
a local minimum. It is the unique global minimum height zero at one negative two.

47
00:05:04,467 --> 00:05:07,267
That's the real power of contour plots.

48
00:05:07,947 --> 00:05:13,867
They let us see a hidden landscape from above, compare heights without leaving two dimensions

49
00:05:14,427 --> 00:05:17,467
and spot where interesting points might lie.

50
00:05:18,147 --> 00:05:24,467
The contour help us discover, the calculation helps us verify and the proof tells when we've

51
00:05:25,147 --> 00:05:26,587
truly reach the bottom.

52
00:05:27,347 --> 00:05:28,167
Thank you for watching.
